      "label": "It has no vertical asymptotes",
      "correct": false
    }
  ],
  "primaryAnswer": "A",
  "alternativeAnswers": [],
  "acceptanceMode": "exact",
  "hint": "$\\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$, and a fraction blows up when its DENOMINATOR is zero.",
  "hints": [
    {
      "level": "nudge",
      "text": "$\\tan x = \\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$, and a fraction blows up when its DENOMINATOR is zero."
    },
    {
      "level": "guide",
      "text": "So ask: for which $x$ is $\\cos x = 0$?"
    },
    {
      "level": "reveal",
      "text": "Cosine is zero at $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and then every $\\pi$ after that: $x = \\frac{\\pi}{2} + k\\pi$."
    }
  ],
  "explanation": [
    "Write tangent as $\\frac{\\sin x}{\\cos x}$.",
    "The function is undefined exactly where $\\cos x = 0$, which happens at $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, $\\frac{3\\pi}{2}$, $-\\frac{\\pi}{2}$, and so on.",
    "All of those are captured by $x = \\frac{\\pi}{2} + k\\pi$, and the graph shoots off to infinity at each one."
  ],
  "alternativeExplanations": [
    {
      "title": "A vertical line has no slope",
      "steps": [
        "$\\tan x$ is the slope of the ray at angle $x$.",
        "At $90^\\circ$ that ray is straight up, and a vertical line has no slope at all.",
        "That undefined slope is exactly the asymptote — and it repeats every half-turn."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "tags": [
    "tangent",
    "asymptotes",
    "domain"
  ],
